import{_ as o,o as r,c as a,a2 as t}from"./chunks/framework.D66Xq0Kv.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Managing DNS records","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/dns.md","filePath":"guide/dns.md"}'),s={name:"guide/dns.md"};function n(i,e,d,c,h,l){return r(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t('<h1 id="managing-dns-records" tabindex="-1">Managing DNS records <a class="header-anchor" href="#managing-dns-records" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Managing DNS records&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The <strong>DNS</strong> page lists every record of the Cloudflare zones your workspace reaches, and lets you add, change and delete them without leaving Orbit. It needs <a href="./cloudflare#connecting">Cloudflare connected</a>, either a token for the workspace or <a href="./cloudflare#shared">one shared by every workspace</a> with zones assigned to it.</p><h2 id="nameservers" tabindex="-1">Using Cloudflare for a domain bought elsewhere <a class="header-anchor" href="#nameservers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Using Cloudflare for a domain bought elsewhere {#nameservers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Your domain can stay registered where you bought it, such as Squarespace, GoDaddy or Namecheap. Only its nameservers move to Cloudflare:</p><ol><li>In Cloudflare, add the domain on the Free plan and check that the imported records match the ones at your registrar. Imports miss uncommon names, so compare them one by one, including <code>MX</code> and <code>TXT</code> records for email and verification.</li><li>If DNSSEC is on at the registrar, turn it off first and wait until the <code>DS</code> record is gone. Changing nameservers with DNSSEC on makes the domain stop resolving.</li><li>At the registrar, replace its nameservers with the two Cloudflare gives you. The change takes from minutes to a few hours, and the domain keeps answering meanwhile because both sides hold the same records.</li><li>Once Cloudflare shows the zone as <strong>Active</strong>, connect a token that includes it, or update the one Orbit uses.</li></ol><p>Registrars such as Squarespace have no API for DNS, so Orbit cannot change records there itself.</p><h2 id="records" tabindex="-1">What the page shows <a class="header-anchor" href="#records" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the page shows {#records}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Pick a zone at the top. Each record shows its type, name, value, proxy and TTL, and:</p><ul><li><strong>The server it reaches</strong>, for address records that point at one of your servers, with its health.</li><li><strong>Orbit · application</strong>, for the records Orbit keeps for a custom domain, the previews of a project or the console address. The link opens the application.</li><li><strong>A warning</strong> when the record sends visitors to a server that is not answering, to a server that does not <a href="./servers#entry">receive traffic</a>, or when the domain Orbit keeps it for is still waiting to be verified.</li></ul><p>Zones the token can only read are marked <strong>read only</strong>. Their records, and record types the page does not edit (such as <code>SRV</code>), show a lock: change them in Cloudflare.</p><h2 id="editing" tabindex="-1">Adding and changing records <a class="header-anchor" href="#editing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Adding and changing records {#editing}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Add record</strong> creates an <code>A</code>, <code>AAAA</code>, <code>CNAME</code>, <code>MX</code>, <code>TXT</code> or <code>CAA</code> record. Type the name relative to the zone (<code>@</code> for the zone itself, or <code>www</code>), or a full hostname inside it. For an <code>A</code> record, the dialog lists your servers so you can point the name at one of them.</p><ul><li><strong>Proxy:</strong> only <code>A</code>, <code>AAAA</code> and <code>CNAME</code> records go through the Cloudflare proxy. Keep <strong>DNS only</strong> for names Orbit verifies and gets certificates for, since Orbit compares the address with your servers.</li><li><strong>TTL:</strong> Auto, or between one minute and one day. Proxied records always use Auto.</li><li><strong>The type</strong> of an existing record cannot change. Delete it and add a new one.</li><li><strong>Comments</strong> starting with <code>Orbit </code> are reserved for the records Orbit manages.</li></ul><h2 id="managed" tabindex="-1">Records Orbit manages <a class="header-anchor" href="#managed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Records Orbit manages {#managed}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Orbit keeps the records of your domains matching the servers that receive traffic, so you rarely need to touch them. Changing or deleting one asks you to confirm, and then Orbit stops managing the DNS of that name: it no longer updates its records when your servers change, and the domain is checked against public DNS again. If the records stop matching what the application needs, the domain&#39;s card says so, and <strong>Configure automatically</strong> puts Orbit back in charge.</p><h2 id="server-down" tabindex="-1">When a server goes down <a class="header-anchor" href="#server-down" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When a server goes down {#server-down}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A server that stops answering stays in DNS: Orbit does not take it out by itself. Visitors whose resolver picks its address get no reply until it comes back, and the page flags those records so you can see them at a glance. Point the names elsewhere while the server is down, or put a proxy with health checks or <a href="./tunnel">Cloudflare Tunnel</a> in front of the applications that must keep answering.</p>',17)])])}const m=o(s,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
